    "title": "A Belated End of 2025 Post - by Ashley Reese",
    "text": "I’ve started pinning a bunch of Cornball Cool Girl Motivational Content™ on Pinterest, and I wish I were a little more embarrassed about it. I’m talking the kind of stuff some 19-year-old probably made in five minutes on her phone and posted on Twitter or TikTok or something, and the subtext of it is all probably “I’m really stressed out about school!!!” or “Time to get straight A’s!!!!” or “I n d to start working out this spring!!!” and here I am, 35-years-old, saving this shit and thinking, “so true!!!”\nI mean, just pinned a graphic someone made of Samantha from Sex and the City with a speech bubble saying, “Can you lock the fuck in?”\nWell, I need to, Samantha from Sex and the City ! I’m ready to .\nOh, wait, where have I heard that before? Right, my Substack post from exactly one year ago .\nIt’s humbling to look back at that post and see all the optimism I had for 2025, and to realize it didn’t really feel like my year as I’d imagined. This was not my “Binz” year . The biggest disappointment that looms over my head every day is the draft of my memoir, which I was sure I’d complete this year, but will likely finish by spring of 2026 instead.\nIt’s really easy to define a year not by what you accomplished, but rather by what you didn’t, by what went wrong. Fair enough: I think sadness has more of an impact on us than happiness. You could get married and get a cushy new job, but who gives a shit about that if your sister gets sick and dies? Your year is going to be defined by how awful it was to watch your sister die, not by how much fun you had on your honeymoon.\n(I feel this way about my wedding sometimes. What a beautiful thing to have happened under such fucked up circumstances, and it is ultimately overshadowed by the fact that I spent nearly a year watching Rob’s body slowly, painfully shut down. That was a bad year.)\nNothing happens cleanly. Tragedy and joy often overlap. Failures brush up against accomplishments. A few good weeks morph into a few bad months, then bounce back. Maybe hoping for Good Years is a child’s way of looking at the world, and adulthood is meant to harden you to the fact that years are just 365 random days you will never actually be prepared for, no matter how level-headed your New Year’s resolutions are.\nBut let me be diplomatic: I’m not going to describe 2025 as either good or bad, but rather strange . I’m choosing to see it as a transitional year, which might be cope (it’s definitely cope), but I don’t care, that’s how I’m going to frame it! For a slew of reasons—some of which will remain private—this year was weird and life-changing and put me in a state of decision paralysis that I’m just now clawing my way out of.\nI didn’t meet my writing goals this year—neither for my book nor for this newsletter—which makes me feel like a failure, because I’m literally a writer . It’s who I am, it’s what I do. I do it for fun. I do it professionally. It is all I know. Who am I if I can’t bring myself to do the thing I’m meant to do?\nStill, I can’t sit here and act like 2025 wasn’t riddled with great moments. I got to travel through Europe for a month . I ate seafood on the beach in Greece. I treated my parents to a trip to Paris and Amsterdam. I saw Oasis in a rainy field in Manchester with my friends.\nI had a giant 35th birthday party at a venue I rented out for my friends. I hired my girl Bao to take photos, got my favorite local DJ, Nick Marc, to spin tracks, got my makeup done by the talented Mollie Gloss , and had a beautiful cake made (thank you, Michelle from Here For Cakes ). Aside from the fact that some dude stole the party favors from off my stoop… the party went off without a hitch. It was the first time I planned such a big event on my own, and it all worked out.\nbao ngo\nbao ngo\nbao ngo And in last year’s post, I was still fretting over my decision to stop taking Lexapro. A couple of weeks ago, I started taking it again. I think it was the right move. There’s no failure in going back to a medication that worked. So, that, and finally getting on top of my fertility journey? There was some personal growth in the last year. Please clap.\nDespite everything I just said about the futility of good years vs bad years, I’m looking forward to 2026. I can’t help it. I love a fresh start, a clean slate, like a new notebook that I may or may not use. I won’t go so far as to declare that 2026 will be my year because I don’t want to jinx it (hell, a comet could hit Earth the day after I turn in my book draft). But I’m going into 2026 determined to feel better about myself and, importantly, to feel like a writer again.\nAlso… I’m going to be absolutely real with you guys: a good chunk of my optimism is due to a tarot reading I received a couple of weeks ago.\nI know this sounds very woo woo. But we’re all allowed to dabble in a little bit of woo woo, as a treat.\nA close friend of mine recently got into Tarot. When I was out in LA for the holidays, she brought her deck to dinner at my request. The Tarot heads reading this can correct me if I’m wrong, but my understanding is that a tarot reading doesn’t necessarily predict the future, but instead acts as a mirror that can reveal hidden patterns, subconscious thoughts, etc. It’s a tool of self-reflection, and you can choose what to do from there.\nSo I sat there, a little skeptical (mostly worried), until the cards were splayed out before me, my friend regularly interjecting with “oh!” and “interesting!” and “wow!” I had no idea what I was looking at, but I was informed that a few major arcana cards were drawn, which apparently represent significant life events. My major arcana cards: Wheel of Fortune, The Lovers, the Empress, and the Hanged Man.\nAs my friend explained each card that was drawn, I’d stare at it, then back at her, then back at it again, in complete disbelief. Together, this tarot portrait accurately described the grief, a tragic romance, my creative roadblocks, and my drive to create—as both a writer and, hopefully, a mother.\nThere were two cards in particular that I was most focused on: The nine of swords and the Empress cards.\nThe Nine of Swords depicts a woman sitting up in bed, head in her hands, swords hanging above her. My friend told me what the card represented, but she didn’t even have to. Dread, anxiety, and anguish. Sleeplessness. I recognized her all too well because she is me. She was my grief in a nutshell. Then there was the Empress card: Fertility, creation, abundance, life. Of course, so much of this stuff is how you interpret it in your own life, and I couldn’t help but see this as particularly on the nose as I begin scheduling fertility appointments and focus on finishing my book.\nMy friend eventually drew my cards for 2026: the Strength card (resilience, courage, overcoming challenges) and the Star card (healing, renewal, hope for the future).\n“The cards don’t lie,” she said, shrugging.\nI believe her.\nSo here’s to at least striving for a “Binz” year in 2026, whether it happens or not.\nAnd here are some of my ins and outs for 2026:\nIn:\nOut:\nBedrotting\nSaying you have “imposter syndrome”\nBringing your dog everywhere (unless it’s a service dog)\nRegime change\nStan culture\nAdding protein to literally everything\nForgetting to text back (me @ me)\nNot having a TV\nBeing camera shy\nFetishizing autism\nLater ♡\nSource link",
